When media at least 5 seconds long starts playing audibly, Ladybird
makes its document’s media session the active one, iframes included,
and a paused session stays active with no time limit. Chromium works
the same way: MediaSessionImpl::ComputeFrameForRouting() routes to
iframes too, the latest audio-focus request wins (AudioFocusManager::
MaybeUpdateActiveSession()), and media under 5 seconds takes only
transient focus (DurationToMediaContentType()). Gecko picks the
document that most recently claimed audible, controllable media
(ShouldClaimActiveAudibleControllableContextForInfo()), with a 3-second
minimum, and drops a paused controller after 24 hours. WebKit ranks
candidates by main-content size and then by the latest user interaction
(selectBestMediaSession()).

Ladybird’s actual playback state is “playing” when the page declares
it, and the guessed state otherwise. Chromium’s MediaSessionImpl::
GetMediaSessionInfoSync() and Gecko’s MediaStatusManager::
UpdateActualPlaybackState() do the same. WebKit instead reports the
playback state of the media element it selected.

Ladybird derives the position from the media only while one element
is controlled, as Chromium’s RebuildAndNotifyMediaPositionChanged()
and Gecko’s MediaStatusManager::GetCurrentPositionState() do.
It doesn’t report drift under 100ms, the threshold in Chromium’s
WebMediaPlayerImpl::MediaPositionNeedsUpdate(). And as in Chromium’s
MediaSession::setActionHandler() and Gecko’s MediaController::
sDefaultSupportedKeys, clearing a handler in Ladybird brings back that
action’s default behavior.

Ladybird keeps MediaStream media out of the selection, as Chromium
does (WebMediaPlayerMS players are one-shot, which MediaSessionImpl::
IsControllable() rejects) and as Gecko does (HTMLMediaElement::
IsControllableMediaSource() rejects a source stream). WebKit
admits such media only once the page sets metadata or a handler
(MediaElementSession::hasNowPlayingInfo()). Media that isn’t in any
document counts in Ladybird, as in Chromium, which registers players
whatever their place in the DOM.

These comparisons come from reading each engine’s source, at Chromium
8658d682eac5, WebKit 047a16c96ccd, and Gecko 06fa2674be3d: What an
engine hands the OS media controls isn’t observable from page script, so
it couldn’t be compared by running them.
